          %meta{:content => "application/xhtml+xml;charset=utf-8", "http-equiv" => "Content-type"}/
          %title
          %p
            This entry is about the inner workings of a web-based program that generates componentwise illustrations of simple
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            as developed at
          %ul
            %li
              J. Paine,
              %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/cgi-bin/webcats/webcats.php"} Category Theory Demonstrations
          %hr/
          %p In this section, I’ll explain coproducts by showing you a program that implements the categorical coproduct of sets. We programmers live our working lives surrounded by data structures and subroutines, entities that become as concrete to us — as “thing-like”, as “manipulable” — as teacups and bricks. So if I can implement some categorical calculations as programs, I hope it will give them a sense of reality that the maths alone might lack. This way of explaining category theory isn’t new, actually: it’s the idea behind the following book, which implements categorical constructions in the programming language ML:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.manchester.ac.uk/~david/categories/book/book.pdf"}>
                %em Computational Category Theory
                (dead link)
              , by
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.manchester.ac.uk/~david/"} David Rydeheard
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.dcs.ed.ac.uk/home/rb/"} Rod Burstall
          %h2#preliminaries_functional_programming_in_python_30 Preliminaries: functional programming in Python 3.0
          %p
            I’ll implement my categorical coproduct in the programming language
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.python.org/download/releases/3.0/"} Python 3.0
          %p
            But most importantly, Python supports
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Functional_programming"} functional programming
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First-class_object"} first-class citizens
          %p In the next few paragraphs, I’ll introduce Python 3.0 and then functional programming in Python. I’ll do so by using it as a desk calculator, where I type commands — usually, expressions to be evaluated — and get back a result. If you want to try this, you can type them either into the Python command-line window, or into Python’s IDLE graphical user-interface window.
          %p As a start, this shows Python evaluating simple arithmetic expressions. What I type is after the three greater-than signs; Python’s reply is on a line by itself:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> 1
                1
                >>> 1+3
                4
                >>> 1+2*5
                11          %p
            I can store a result in a variable, and then use the variable as all or part of an expression. I’m showing you this because although the example below only stores numbers in variables, I shall later want to store functions. Please note that
            %code
              eleven = 1+2
              %em 5
            means “put the result of evaluating
            %code
              1+2
              %em 5
            into the variable
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %code eleven
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> eleven = 1+2*5
                >>> eleven
                11
                >>> eleven * 2
                22          %p
            I can call a function. The
            %code from math import *
            below merely gets me the built-in arithmetic functions, including
            = succeed "," do
              %code exp
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> from math import *
                >>> exp(1)
                2.7182818284590451          %p
            This is where it begins getting interesting. Below, I put the
            %em function
            %code exp
            into the variable
            = succeed "." do
              %code myfunc
            %code myfunc
            in the same way I did
            = succeed ":" do
              %code exp
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> myfunc = exp
                >>> myfunc(1)
                2.7182818284590451          %p
            Instead of using
            %code exp
            as a function value, I can write a “lambda expression”:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> identity = lambda x: x
                >>> identity(5)
                5
                >>> double = lambda x: 2*x
                >>> double(5)
                10          %p
            A lambda expression denotes a function without naming it or hiving it off into a separate definition. Then, in the same way that 1 denotes an integer and can be used as a constant in a language of arithmetic expressions, a lambda expression can be used in a language of expressions over functions. You can see this in the interaction below, where I have applied a lambda expression to 5. The syntax is the same as for
            = succeed "," do
              %code double(5)
            = succeed "," do
              %code double
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> (lambda x: 2*x)(5)
                10          %p
            By the way, lambda expressions arose as part of the lambda calculus. [ Possibly put some of this on a new lambda calculus page ] This is a mathematical model of computation, invented by the logician
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alonzo_Church"} Alonzo Church
            as a foundation for logic. (One topic he wanted to investigate was Gödel’s incompleteness theorem, hoping it might not apply to logic founded on the lambda calculus.) It consists of a language of expressions over values which include functions, and a set of transformation rules for evaluating these expressions. His notation for functions used Greek lambda to signal a function’s argument: in it, the identity function is written
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.x
            = succeed "," do
              %code lambda x: x
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/10/classical_vs_quantum_computati.html"}
                %em Classical vs Quantum Computation (Week 1)
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www-maths.swan.ac.uk/staff/jrh/papers/JRHHislamWeb.pdf"}>
                %em History of Lambda-calculus and Combinatory Logic
              , by Felice Cardone and J. Roger Hindley.
          %p … I now have all the prerequisites needed for some functional programming….
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                >>> compose = lambda f, g: lambda x: g(f(x))
                
                >>> double_then_exp = compose( double, exp )
                >>> double_then_exp(1)
                7.3890560989306504
                >>> exp(2)
                7.3890560989306504           %h2#categorical_definition_of_coproduct Categorical definition of coproduct
          %p
            I’ll start with the definition of coproduct, which I’ve lightly edited from Section 3,
            %em Coproducts and Colimits
            in Chapter III.
            = succeed "," do
              %em Universals and Limits
          %ul
            %li [Categories for the Working Mathematician], by Saunders Mac Lane.
          %blockquote
            %p
              For any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %em coproduct diagram
              consists of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} objects
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:a\to c
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo :
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:b\to c
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:a\to d
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:b\to d
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:c\to d
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mo ;
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = i;h
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mi j
                      %mo ;
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = j;h
          %p
            Before going on to an example, I must just explain that I am using semicolon to denote composition, with the meaning that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ;
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i;h)(x)
            \=
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(i(x))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            first, then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            explains, this is an alternative to the standard mathematical notation
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \circ i
          %p
            To create an example of a coproduct diagram, I’ll start by defining the sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                a = { 'dimanche', 'lundi', 'mardi' };
                b = { 'Mittwoch', 'Donnerstag', 'Freitag', 'Samstag' };
                c = { 'Sunday', 'Monday', 'Tuesday', 'Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday', 'Saturday' };          %p Python 3.0 has adopted mathematical notation for sets, enclosing them in curly brackets. Quotes enclose string constants, which I’m using to create arbitrary set elements.
          %p
            Now I need the pair of arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                # Maps a to c.
                def i( x ): 
                  if x == 'dimanche':
                    return 'Sunday';
                  elif x == 'lundi':
                    return 'Monday';
                  elif x == 'mardi':
                    return 'Tuesday'; 
                
                # Maps b to c.
                def j( x ): 
                  if x == 'Mittwoch':
                    return 'Wednesday';
                  elif x == 'Donnerstag':
                    return 'Thursday';
                  elif x == 'Freitag':
                    return 'Friday';
                  elif x == 'Samstag':
                    return 'Saturday';          %p …[Explain somewhere why I have made a and b not subsets of c. In examples where they are, perhaps readers don’t see the purpose of the injections, so I want to make that explicit. Also, I shall later use this [but maybe not appropriate topic for nLab? Though relevant to HDA&amp;N] to talk about “glueing data” (database merging, sensor fusion.)]
          %p
            One important point is that, to keep my program simple, I’m cheating a little bit. In category theory, essential parts of every arrow are its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            and its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            that maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is
            %em different
            from an arrow that maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            into — say — the set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Sunday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Monday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Tuesday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{'Sunday','Monday','Tuesday'\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Sunday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Monday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Tuesday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Friday
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{'Sunday','Monday','Tuesday','Friday'\}
          %p
            If I represent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            by functions as above, I can’t represent that in my code. But I shall do so by writing the source and target in a comment above every function. [Perhaps I’ll show a version later that does represent arrows. Use a dict with source, target, and body fields.]
          %p Now I’ll show the definition of coproduct again:
          %blockquote
            %p
              For any category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %em coproduct diagram
              consists of objects
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:a\to c
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo :
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:b\to c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:a\to d
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:b\to d
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:c\to d
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mo ;
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = i;h
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mi j
                      %mo ;
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = j;h
          %p
            I have created the objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            and the arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          %blockquote
            %p
              You give me any pair of arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:a\to d
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:b\to d
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:c\to d
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mo ;
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = i;h
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mi j
                      %mo ;
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = j;h
          %p
            But this sounds like a function talking! “You give me these arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            = succeed ",\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            and returns our
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
          %p …[To be continued]
